Context
  Asked to remove portclaim from public reference in this repo unless needed, on
  the grounds that the skill needs local plugin data and not an external service,
  and to convert anything referencing 192.168 into a concept. Then asked to check
  the plugin manifest against the manifest-reference spec.

  The repository is PUBLIC (gh repo view: private=false), which is what makes the
  address in the committed artifacts worth acting on rather than tidying. The
  shipped skill was already clean: eval 4 asserts nothing is sent off-box, and
  SKILL.md named no endpoint. What remained was generated evidence plus one line
  of guidance recommending the operator record a port somewhere.

Deviations
  Redacted rather than deleted, and recorded the redaction. b9600a6's note
  reasoned that scrubbing a transcript falsifies evidence; that reasoning is not
  wrong, so REDACTIONS.org states what was substituted and why. The consequence
  is honest but slightly odd reading: the with_skill arm's real GET against a
  real service now appears to probe a placeholder.

  evals/PLAN.org's remaining hydra.lan references are on the other branch and
  were left in place: no address, the IP is already recorded as operator-held and
  absent, and the FQDN-vs-short-name distinction IS the known_hosts finding.
  PLAN.org also publishes an SSH host-key fingerprint -- a public key, not a
  secret, and load-bearing to "this is not a key mismatch" -- flagged rather than
  removed, because that is a judgement call for the operator.

Considerations
  This branch carries skills/ and .claude-plugin/ changes, so it is `deployable':
  merging it publishes a release. That is why it is separate from the roadmap and
  direnv work, which touches nothing shipped.

  0.1.5 skips 0.1.4 on purpose. PR #4 (feat/skill-compatibility) already sets
  0.1.4 and is unmerged; two branches claiming one version is exactly the failure
  docs/roadmap.org files as C7 -- check-version-bump.sh only greps for an added
  "version" line, so a collision passes review and fails publish.yml on "tag
  already exists" after merge. Skipping a number is free.

  Adopting --strict in three places is a small ratchet: any future manifest
  warning now fails gmake checks, CI and release staging. That was the documented
  intent (CONTRIBUTING.org:33), and the manifest only just became warning-free.

Clarifications
  `icon' was referenced nowhere else in the tree -- the only mention was the key
  itself -- so moving it under metadata keeps the asset recorded without
  pretending Claude Code reads it. assets/icon.svg is untouched.

Timeline
  2026-09-28, nexus, FreeBSD 15.1-RELEASE, guile3 3.0.10, Claude Code CLI as
  installed, as dsp-dr. Branched from main. gmake checks green: 3 eval suites
  valid, 7/7 proxy regressions, validate --strict passing.

Amended 2026-09-29
  Rebased onto origin/main after PRs #4 and #5 merged (02:20) and v0.1.4 was
  tagged and published. One conflict, on plugin.json's version line, exactly as
  the pre-rebase dry run predicted; SKILL.md auto-merged, so PR #4's
  `compatibility' frontmatter and this branch's port-bullet rewording coexist.

  0.1.5 is no longer a skipped number. It was chosen when 0.1.4 was held by an
  unmerged PR and skipping looked cheaper than colliding; 0.1.4 has since been
  published, so 0.1.5 is simply the next one. The reasoning is now written up as
  the version rule in RELEASING.org on the other branch.

  Re-verified after the rebase: validate --strict passes, gmake checks is green
  (3 eval suites, 7/7 proxy regressions), the generated .md files regenerate to
  no diff, check-version-bump.sh against origin/main reports the bump, and no
  tracked or untracked file references a private address or the registry by name.
